Ron Howard knows his way around a camera: in front of and behind the lens; scripted film or documentary feature. But when his production company, Imagine Entertainment (co-chaired by Brian Grazer), was considering a biodoc on fashion photographer Richard Avedon , Howard wasn&#x2019;t sure if he should be at the helm.

&#x201C;I really went into it thinking, &#x2018;This is a great subject, it'd be great for Imagine,&#x2019;&#x201D; Howard said of the Avedon Foundation opening up the artist&#x2019;s archives to him during a screening at SCAD Film Festival on Oct. 28. &#x201C;As I began sort of seeing the breadth of that work, I was immediately impressed, but I still felt like, I'm not sure I'm really the right filmmaker for this, but this is fascinating and I bet we could explore what went on behind the scenes making many of these great photos that we remember." blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Beyond the Book From Book Club to Netflix: How Katherine Center's 'The Bodyguard' Became 'Guarding Stars' Movies Lee Chang-dong on the Mysteries and Meaning of 'Possible Love'

&#x201C;As I began to do some research, soon I really did connect with it as this very human story that I wanted to tell,&#x201D; Howard continued. &#x201C;It was less anecdotal about how photos were done and what glamorous or intriguing figures sat for him, and much, much more about an artist's journey, and I related to it. In some ways, we&#x2019;re very different people &#x2014; he's an extrovert, I'm not; he was the life of the party, I avoid them &#x2014; but, man, did he have drive and ambition.&#x201D;

The resulting film, Avedon , now streaming on Netflix , blends commentary from collaborators &#x2014; former magazine editor Tina Brown, model Lauren Hutton and dancer and choreographer Twyla Tharp &#x2014; with Richard&#x2019;s works, both commercial and historic, like his portraits of figures of the civil rights movement. The project heavily explores Avedon&#x2019;s legacy as an artist with strong creative and political convictions, as well as his struggle balancing those passions with his obligations as a husband and father. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;Ultimately, we had 500 photographs in the documentary, but our amazing producing partner, Dallas Brennan Rexer, combed through 16,000 sittings of Avedon,&#x201D; producer Sara Bernstein, president of Imagine Documentaries, explained during the Q&A. &#x201C;Initially, we thought there might be a select number of hero images that would take us through Avedon's creative story. But then there's this backdrop of, essentially, the back half of the 20th century, so you could match these images with these different moments in history or these different personalities who represent different buckets of history. And then you were able to see a deeper, personal story when John Avedon sat down for us.&#x201D;

Richard&#x2019;s son, John, whose son, Michael, is also a fashion photographer, was a &#x201C;reluctant interview,&#x201D; despite being supportive of the project, Howard noted as he shared how he got John on board as a talking head.

&#x201C;Frankly, I said to him, &#x2018;There's a gap here because we have your father's voice. We have people who worked with him, but we don't have that intimate connection, that deeper understanding that's away from the studio.&#x2019; And I think as a writer himself, he recognized that there was a role for him to fill,&#x201D; said Howard, later adding, &#x201C;now [the film] became a generational thing about fathers and sons in addition to everything else.&#x201D;

In one of the final scenes of the film, Avedon, who died in 2001, makes a startlingly reticent prediction about the outlook on his art form, stating, "I don't think there will be photography in the future, only machines." blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Asked what he thinks Avedon would capture at this moment in time, where the battle between art and AI seems to be reaching its peak, Howard said, &#x201C;He'd probably still be shooting on the white paper and probably still using that three-by-five camera because, for him, it did become a kind of communication and an exploration, capturing those moments. I think he would still be entirely relevant because he became the story behind the story as an artist. And we began to recognize his work; whether we knew it was Avedon, we could feel the hand of a creator."

Added Howard of how Richard might approach AI, &#x201C;My guess is also with his sensibility and aesthetic, he'd be using it as a tool to advance his aesthetic. I don't think he'd be necessarily running away from it, but he would want to stay in control.&#x201D;

Watch the full Q&A in the video above.

Many of documentary&#x2019;s leading lights gathered in San Francisco this month for Doc Stories, the prestigious four-day festival put on by SFFilm. Oscar winner Laura Poitras, two-time Oscar winner Ben Proudfoot, Oscar nominees Ava DuVernay and Sam Pollard, Sundance directing award winner J.M. Harper and more came to the City by the Bay for what has emerged as an increasingly important event on the nonfiction film calendar.

For a third year in a row, Deadline&#x2019;s Doc Talk podcast joined the festivities. On the latest edition of the pod, we visit with Anne Lai, executive director of SFFilm, and director of programming Jessie Fairbanks for a conversation about the 12th edition of Doc Stories. The festival leaders tell us why they limit the event to a tightly curated lineup of 10 features and two shorts blocs. We also get into the breakout success of the Elizabeth Holmes-focused documentary You Can See Everything and whether it will be embraced by Oscar documentary branch voters, who sometimes sniff at stylistic innovations such as those deployed in Nathan Fielder and Lance Oppenheim's film. Lai tells us why she likens the doc branch to the Electoral College &#x2014; an institution noted for its power to upend the popular vote. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article1-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid","mid-articleX","mid-article","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[2,2],[300,250],[620,350],[300,251],[501,282],[3,3],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

We also spend time with Pollard to explore his Oscar-contending documentary Tutu , about the late Archbishop Desmond Tutu, who won the Nobel Peace Prize for helping to bring down South Africa&#x2019;s racist system of apartheid. As his film documents, President Nelson Mandela handed an exceedingly difficult task to the archbishop after white rule fell &#x2013; leading a Truth and Reconciliation Commission to come to terms with South Africa&#x2019;s long and brutal history of strict racial segregation. Pollard tells us why he believes the U.S. will never have its own Truth and Reconciliation Commission. Watch on Deadline if ( !window.pmc.harmony?.isEventAdScheduledTime() ) { pmcCnx.cmd.push(function() { pmcCnx({ settings: { plugins: { pmcAtlasMG: { iabPlcmt: 2, } } }, playerId: '32fe25c4-79aa-406a-af44-69b41e969e71', playlistId: '0199e506-e642-7728-8264-463efe71337b', }).render("connatix_contextual_player_div"); }); } else { // This should only be get called when page cache is not cleared and it's event time. window.pmc.harmony?.switchToHarmonyPlayer(); }

Harper joins Doc Talk along with Pollard, discussing his award-winning documentary Soul Patrol that examines a super-elite all-Black unit in the Vietnam War. At the age of 14, Harper became fascinated by the war, even though it had long since ended by the time he was born. The filmmaker explains why the war has absorbed his focus for so many years, and how his documentary grew out of reading the memoir Soul Patrol , written by Vietnam veteran Ed Emanuel. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dl-mid-article-inject2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article2","mid-articleX","mid-article","mid","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

That&#x2019;s on the new episode of Doc Talk hosted by Oscar winner John Ridley ( 12 Years a Slave , Shirley ) and Matt Carey, Deadline&#x2019;s senior documentary editor. The pod is a production of Deadline and Ridley&#x2019;s N&#x14D; Studios.

Listen to the episode above or on major podcast platforms including Spotify , iHeart and Apple .

When Czech documentarian and photographer Hana Jakrlov&#xE1; encountered the crumbling streets of Cuba after meeting the dissident punk musician Gorki there, she had a strangely familiar sensation.

&#x201C;I was living in New York at the time,&#x201D; she recalls, &#x201C;and Obama had just begun the process of warming relations with Cuba. All my friends in New York were worried about Cuba changing and McDonald's popping up on every corner.&#x201D; Related Stories Skydance to Fold Paramount TV Studios Into Warner Bros. TV Under Channing Dungey and Matt Thunell Rob Lowe Asked Ben Shapiro if He Could Star in 'Confession' After Reading 'Fantastic' Script Despite Being Considered 'Way Too Handsome' for Role

&#x201C;So I went to photograph Cuba &#x2013; and experienced the strangest sense of d&#xE9;j&#xE0; vu. I had spent the first 20 years of my life in communist Czechoslovakia, and being in Cuba felt like stepping back into my youth, only in a Caribbean setting. Everything was familiar: the hopelessness, the absurdity, the propaganda, the misery &#x2013; both economic and, above all, spiritual.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Then, she says, she met a Cuban punk guitar player and leader of the banned group Porno para Ricardo, leading to a 12-year production journey that culminated this week in her feature docu debut, &#x201C;Porno Para Ricardo,&#x201D; which screened recently at the Ji.hlava International Documentary Film Festival.

&#x201C;After talking to him and attending a Porno para Ricardo rehearsal (at Gorki's house &#x2013; the band had been completely driven underground by the Cuban regime and could no longer perform in public), I realized I wanted to make a project about this guy.&#x201D;

At first as part of Jakrlov&#xE1;&#x2019;s photography project on Cuban anti-regime artists, she soon realized she needed to concentrate on Gorki and his band, she says.

&#x201C;I also came to understand that something was missing from my photographs of a punk band. So I started filming, initially with my own camera and later with a director of photography.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

That was in 2014, the director says, adding she&#x2019;s now been to Cuba 10 times and spent almost a year there in total. "One thing that has always remained a mystery to me is how the establishment kept letting me back in.&#x201D;

Jakrlov&#xE1; sometimes filmed herself and also used archival material &#x201C;shot by the brilliant Cuban documentary filmmaker and dissident Claudio Fuentes.&#x201D;

But she brought in respected Czech cinematographer Adam Ol&#x2019;ha to help capture the remarkably striking visuals of Gorki&#x2019;s life and struggles.

&#x201C;We also worked with local talent &#x2013; sound people and others &#x2013; and some of them faced serious repercussions for their courage in working with us.&#x201D;

&#x201C;I wanted to show the struggle of a free spirit trapped in a cage,&#x201D; Jakrlov&#xE1; says. &#x201C;The life of an artist and dissident who has dedicated his entire life to fighting a totalitarian regime, spent three years in prison and faces persecution and oppression on a daily basis. But I also wanted to show a man torn between his love for his country and his love for his family. Gorki has a five-year-old grandson who lives in exile with his daughter, and Gorki has never been able to see him.&#x201D;

Then, things changed, thanks to Cuban authorities, she says. &#x201C;The regime gave him a clear ultimatum: Either he leaves the country or he goes to prison.&#x201D;

&#x201C;So, after filming Gorki's dramatic departure into forced exile, the film became not only a punk essay on freedom, but also a film about exile.&#x201D;

Filming her subject in Cuba involved daily harassment and threats from police, Jakrlov&#xE1; says. They also worried about another possibility: That he could die in a mysterious car accident, as has happened to several Cuban dissidents.&#x201D;

The regime worries about its image abroad, says Jakrlov&#xE1;. &#x201C;This helps explain why our crew wasn't arrested during the shoot. However, we were followed constantly, as became apparent during Gorki's subsequent arrest.&#x201D;

As for why Gorki has always remained so passionate about his message (&#x201C;This is for the bitch Raul Castro,&#x201D; he yells while introducing one anti-regime song) Jakrlov&#xE1; says she came to understand they share some core beliefs.

&#x201C;We are both freedom-loving people. Unlike Gorki, I was lucky enough to see communism fall in my country towards the end of my adolescence, so I never had to find out whether I had it in me to fight against it. What motivates Gorki is his love of freedom, his need for freedom, and also his sense of responsibility.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;And that's what connects Gorki with people like V&#xE1;clav Havel, Oswaldo Pay&#xE1; or Nelson Mandela. These were courageous and truly inspirational people. I feel incredibly lucky to have met two of them: Gorki &#xC1;guila and V&#xE1;clav Havel.&#x201D;

Jakrlov&#xE1;&#x2019;s greatest challenge in completing &#x201C;Porno Para Ricardo,&#x201D; she says, was the deep daily dysfunction of Cuban society.

&#x201C;Being a director is challenging, especially in a country where nothing works and nobody expects anything to work.&#x201D;

&#x201C;The only thing that worked 100% was Gorki. He is the most responsible person I know, always ready to work. He even risked his life by waiting for the film crew to arrive in Cuba after the regime had threatened him and given him an ultimatum: Leave his country or go to prison.&#x201D;

Gorki now resides in Mexico, where he is awaiting a decision on a U.S. visa that would reunite him with his wife, daughter and grandson in Florida, Jakrlov&#xE1; says.

Whether it's intertwining their lived experiences with the characters they portray or drawing inspiration from historical events and figures, the women of this year&#x2019;s awards season are using their roles in the entertainment industry to highlight real-life stories. Carrie Coon , Nia Long and more stars gathered at Variety's High Tea: Celebrating the Women of Awards Season event on Monday to kick off awards season.

A Spotlight Conversation with Carrie Coon blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Rob Lowe Asked Ben Shapiro if He Could Star in 'Confession' After Reading 'Fantastic' Script Despite Being Considered 'Way Too Handsome' for Role DOC NYC Doc ‘Dryads,’ From Mallorca-Based Miguel Eek, Explores ‘the Mystery of Creation Through Music’

Coon took the role of Rita Cooper in &#x201C;The Liberation,&#x201D; which tells the story of a woman, inspired by director Guy Nattiv&#x2019;s own grandmother, who is haunted by her past as a Holocaust survivor and overwhelmed by her responsibilities as a wife and mother, ultimately leading her to join an all-female cult. Coon was intrigued by the character because she didn&#x2019;t recognize herself in the script but still found a way to connect with her.

&#x201C;That emotional place is not far from me,&#x201D; Coon said. &#x201C;I&#x2019;m a wife, I&#x2019;m a mother and I have a career. The cognitive load of motherhood is very high. What mother do you know hasn&#x2019;t woken up and said, &#x2018;Boy, I could really just leave. What would happen if I just left?&#x2019; And what&#x2019;s radical about Rita is that she did.&#x201D;

&#x201C;Tangles&#x201D; producer Lauren Miller Rogen experienced the opposite of Coon: She would not be satisfied sharing her mother&#x2019;s battle with Alzheimer&#x2019;s until she saw herself in the script.

&#x201C;One of the main things that I love and look for in movies is for my own story,&#x201D; Rogen said. &#x201C;Maybe that makes me selfish, but I think that&#x2019;s how we connect and how we as humans learn about and share our human experience. I knew that &#x2018;Tangles,&#x2019; even though it was specific, was also incredibly universal.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Jennifer Peedom, the director of the biographical expedition film about Mount Everest called &#x201C;Tensing,&#x201D; agreed with Rogen that even specific stories can create relatability for audiences.

&#x201C;Who in this room has not felt underestimated?&#x201D; Peedom said. &#x201C;That was ultimately what I wanted to put out in the world: to look at this person who had been so overlooked and so underestimated for so much of his life, and I saw something of myself in that.&#x201D;

While &#x201C;Avatar Aang: The Last Airbender" producer Latifa Ouaou wasn&#x2019;t telling a personal story, she recognized the weight of the widespread themes her film dealt with, especially how they are translated in children&#x2019;s media.

&#x201C;It's one of the only action-adventure kids properties that really deals with genocide, multiculturalism versus tribalism and all of these beautiful, heavy themes that you would never be able to get away with working on any other family-style movie at a studio,&#x201D; Ouaou said. &#x201C;That's really what kept me going is that making a meaningful movie within that environment is often really challenging.&#x201D;

The filmmakers of the documentary &#x201C;Nothing to See Here: Watts&#x201D; took a different approach to feeling seen in their film by literally seeing themselves. The film compiles raw footage of members of the Watts, Calif., community, including directors Lawanda Hawkins and Qiuana Williams, filming their lifestyles, revealing the bigger picture and encouraging the community to come together to enact change.

&#x201C;The people in Watts show us what happens when you sit across the table and reveal your most personal, deepest, real self to someone who might even be your enemy and listen to what they have to say. Then something remarkable happens,&#x201D; producer Suzanne Malveaux said. &#x201C;That&#x2019;s what we believe is a model that could be a model for other communities.&#x201D;

Although both Nia Long, who played Katherine Jackson in &#x201C;Michael,&#x201D; and Ruth Madeley, who played Judy Heumann in &#x201C;Being Heumann,&#x201D; were telling the stories of larger-than-life icons who came before them, it was not difficult for them to relate to their characters.

&#x201C;There was a grace and a peace, and a weight that she carried that I didn&#x2019;t really need to research,&#x201D; Long said. &#x201C;I&#x2019;m a Black woman. I know what it&#x2019;s like to walk around this world every single day, and so although my experience is different, there are parts of me that are connected to generational trauma that make my path and my understanding of this life very clear.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Madeley added, &#x201C;[Heumann] changed the world and she did it with an unwillingness to relent. She was like, &#x2018;I will not lose this argument&#x2019; and I have been known to have similar characteristics. I&#x2019;m just like a dog with a bone. I&#x2019;m unashamedly ambitious. I&#x2019;ve been the same since I was a kid, and it&#x2019;s not letting up anytime soon.&#x201D;

Marion Bailey didn&#x2019;t have the challenge of playing a renowned character that audiences are familiar with in her latest movie &#x201C;Tender Loving Care,&#x201D; but she does help tell the multigenerational story of fictional women, imbued with her own story by acting alongside her biological daughter, Alice Bailey Johnson.

&#x201C;This film seems to have somehow hit the zeitgeist,&#x201D; Bailey said. &#x201C;I think that&#x2019;s taken us all by surprise. It seems to be something people need and want, which is a film about human beings caring for each other and looking after each other, which sounds sort of undramatic, but I think it&#x2019;s touched people.&#x201D;

All of these women would not be able to emotionally embody their characters without getting into costume. For Marci Rodgers, the costume director of &#x201C;Michael,&#x201D; dressing characters as venerated as the Jacksons meant curating an extensive 800-page research book.

&#x201C;I really turned into a bit of an investigator, and I like to say that Michael guided me,&#x201D; Rodgers said. &#x201C;It was more so just keeping in mind deference and reverence.&#x201D;

Rodgers also took a moment to acknowledge the impact production designer Barabara Ling had on her experience working on &#x201C;Michael.&#x201D;

&#x201C;She had the ability to make history feel present, but as much as the set meant to me as a Michael fan, there was a particular moment that day that I will carry with me forever as a huge fan of Barbara,&#x201D; Rodgers said. &#x201C;She said, &#x2018;Marci, that jacket and that shirt on Michael is spectacular, like a living light show. Bravo!&#x2019; I looked at her, and she looked at me and said, &#x2018;Good job, kiddo.&#x2019; I will never forget those words.&#x201D;

"The Devil Wears Prada 2" costume designer Molly Rogers had plenty from the first film to reference, and she needed to discover a way to make it equally as timeless. Rogers accomplished this by landing on the iconic red Balenciaga gown. &#x201C;When [Streep] and I met, we looked at each other and said, &#x2018;The devil comes back in red. You&#x2019;re gonna come back in cerulean. I don&#x2019;t think so, right?&#x2019;&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid3' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Rogers added, &#x201C;When you&#x2019;re doing contemporary, especially if it takes place in a fashion magazine, the audience is disappointed if it&#x2019;s a gown from no one, because I could definitely build it, but I think that it&#x2019;s very important in a project like this to have to invite houses to submit what they think and work with them.&#x201D;

Establishing this modern feel was a challenge for Rogers. She didn&#x2019;t want the clothing showcased in the film to feel like an advertisement.

&#x201C;I liked the first one so much because, to me, you couldn&#x2019;t really tell what year it was and I was hoping that I could make the second movie unidentifiable in a way,&#x201D; Rogers said. &#x201C;Now I know the pressure of two is a curse, and I loved the first one so much that I went to Meryl and I said, &#x2018;Do you think from what you&#x2019;ve seen of the clothes, that I&#x2019;m gonna have to go to Brazil and have facial reconstruction?&#x2019; and she said, &#x2018;The jury&#x2019;s out.&#x2019; It was scary to look outside my dressing room because I&#x2019;ve had death threats over putting a hat on Sarah Jessica [Parker] before.&#x201D;

&#x201C;The Odyssey&#x201D; costume designer Ellen Mirojnick similarly had a struggle of working from source material while simultaneously trying to create something new.

&#x201C;Everybody is aware of Greek mythology and what that looks like, but Chris [Nolan] had written a magnificent script and it was an adaptation &#x2014; one should never forget an adaptation of &#x2018;The Odyssey.&#x2019; There was a modernity to it. I knew that we were going to create a new mythology, but I&#x2019;d be damned if I knew exactly what it was going to be.&#x201D;

It didn&#x2019;t take long for Mirojnick to see that they had successfully done so and her ideas had worked when brought to life, especially for Zendaya&#x2019;s character Athena.

&#x201C;Her costume was 100% set, but what had to happen was that she gets her head chopped off in this scene and it's a very important scene and that humbleness of that character had to come across in that moment when that sword comes down,&#x201D; Mirojnick recalled. &#x201C;They put her down on her knees, and they take her — not a stunt double — through the action, and there she was being dragged up the steps, being thrown on the ground and her head is cut off and that was her first day of shooting.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid4' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

On the contrary, Deborah Cook, the costume designer of the stop-motion animated film &#x201C; Wildwood ,&#x201D; had a more time-consuming process, creating up to 44 duplicates of each of her characters&#x2019; costumes to ensure cohesion.

&#x201C;I do everything the same as a live-action costume designer would do,&#x201D; Cook said. &#x201C;I do the research, build the character profiles, their identity and work out where the costume pivots. They tell the narrative in a really subliminal way that weaves through the story. But with stop frame, we shoot 25 frames a second, so we shoot in stills and our characters are small scale, so they&#x2019;re 1/5 scale and we have to engineer all of those costumes to move in a naturalistic way to look like a human-scale person.&#x201D;

While all these talented professionals bring their unique perspectives and lived experiences to their individual projects, there is one thing they all have in common: the profound experience of being a woman. This fact alone, they all concur, has contributed to the challenges they&#x2019;ve faced and the success they have achieved and is worth recognizing and celebrating in media.

&#x201C;At the end of the day, all of us feel the same things, all of us understand the same things and that is the most powerful thing we carry as women,&#x201D; Long said. &#x201C;We have to have more of those conversations in order to create unity and power among all of us to actually keep creating.&#x201D;

