NBC&#x2019;s page program dates back to 1933, during the golden age of radio, and began as a way to assist in guest relations, ushering, tour services and more at the thenbrand new Rockefeller Plaza.

But over the past decade, the NBC page gig has become much more of a structured career-learning experience that acts like a paid apprenticeship with a beginning, middle and end. The highly competitive program selects around 22 to 25 people in New York and 12 to 15 in Los Angeles three times a year for what&#x2019;s become a TV business and NBC boot camp.

According to Priany Hadiatmodjo, senior director of the NBC page program, around 3,000 people apply for each cycle to get one of those coveted spots.

&#x201C;It&#x2019;s a pretty rigorous process to get in,&#x201D; she says. &#x201C;We read every single application. There&#x2019;s a video interview component that comes next, where you have to answer some questions that help us understand who you are. And then the final stage of the application process is a panel interview. The process is not for the faint of heart. A lot of people apply multiple times. Sometimes they get in on their second or even third try. I don&#x2019;t think there&#x2019;ll ever be a shortage of people who are really eager to work in this industry. But once you&#x2019;re here, the expectation to excel is probably harder than the actual process itself.&#x201D;

